    passage: "The principle of linguistic relativity—often called the Sapir-Whorf hypothesis—holds that the language we speak shapes the way we perceive and think about the world. The strong version of this hypothesis, which claims that language determines thought and that speakers of different languages are incapable of understanding each other's concepts, has been largely abandoned; no serious researcher defends it today. The weak version, however, has accumulated considerable experimental support. Studies have shown that speakers of languages with obligatory grammatical gender, such as Spanish or German, tend to associate gendered adjectives with inanimate objects in ways that match their language's categories. Russian speakers, whose language distinguishes multiple shades of blue with separate words, are faster at discriminating between those shades than English speakers are. And the Pirahã people of the Amazon, whose language lacks number words beyond approximations, perform differently on exact-quantity matching tasks. These findings suggest a subtler truth: language does not imprison thought, but it can bias attention, memory, and categorization at the margins.",
    text: "Which choice best describes the author's assessment of linguistic relativity?",
    options: [
      { id: "A", text: "The strong version of the hypothesis has been proven correct by modern experiments." },
      { id: "B", text: "The hypothesis is completely false, and language has no effect whatsoever on cognition." },
      { id: "C", text: "The strong version is discredited, but the weak version is supported by evidence showing language can subtly shape perception and categorization." },
      { id: "D", text: "The Pirahã people's lack of number words proves that language determines which concepts a mind can form." }
    ],
    correctAnswer: "C",
    explanation: "The passage states that the strong version 'has been largely abandoned,' while the weak version 'has accumulated considerable experimental support,' citing the color-discrimination and grammatical-gender studies. Option C captures this nuanced assessment. Option A is contradicted—the strong version is discredited. Option B is contradicted—the passage presents evidence of real effects. Option D misreads the Pirahã example—their performance differs on exact-quantity tasks, but the passage does not claim they lack the concepts.",
