    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The Fermi paradox begins with a simple observation: given the vast number of stars in our galaxy—hundreds of billions—and the apparent abundance of planets capable of supporting life, the galaxy ought to be teeming with intelligent civilizations. Yet humanity has detected no unambiguous signal from any of them. This silence is the paradox named after physicist Enrico Fermi, who is said to have asked, 'Where is everybody?' Proposed resolutions fall into several broad categories. One class of explanations holds that intelligent life is genuinely rare: the conditions required for complex life, let alone technological civilization, may be vanishingly uncommon, and Earth may be nearly unique. A second class suggests that civilizations do arise but destroy themselves before achieving interstellar communication, whether through nuclear war, environmental collapse, or runaway technology. A third class posits that advanced civilizations exist but are deliberately silent, hiding from potential threats or simply uninterested in contacting less advanced species. Each resolution has its adherents, and none has been confirmed—which is precisely why the paradox continues to provoke both scientific and philosophical debate about humanity's place in the cosmos.",
