    id: 1637,
    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The Hawthorne effect takes its name from a series of experiments conducted at the Hawthorne Works factory near Chicago in the 1920s and 1930s. Researchers studying the relationship between workplace conditions and productivity found that workers' output increased whenever the lighting was improved—but also when it was dimmed again. Puzzled, the researchers concluded that the workers were responding not to the physical changes themselves but to the fact that they were being observed and that their working conditions were the focus of attention. The term 'Hawthorne effect' is now used broadly to describe any situation in which research participants modify their behavior because they know they are part of a study. More recent scholarship, however, has questioned the original interpretation. Reanalyses of the Hawthorne data suggest that other factors—such as changes in payment schemes, the departure of particularly slow workers, and the practice of giving workers feedback on their output—may explain much of the productivity gains. The episode has become a cautionary tale about the hazards of drawing sweeping conclusions from imperfectly designed studies.",
