    passage: "The global decline of pollinating insects—bees, butterflies, moths, and other species that transfer pollen between flowers—has been documented on every inhabited continent, and scientists generally agree that no single factor is responsible. Habitat loss tops most lists: the conversion of flower-rich meadows and hedgerows into monoculture farmland removes both nesting sites and the diverse floral resources pollinators need throughout the season. Pesticides, particularly a class of chemicals called neonicotinoids, have been shown to impair the navigation and foraging abilities of bees even at sublethal doses. Climate change shifts flowering times, so that plants and their pollinators, which often evolved in close synchrony, can fall out of step. Finally, pathogens and parasites—most famously the Varroa mite, which attacks honeybee colonies—add further pressure. What makes the situation especially serious is that these factors do not operate independently: a colony weakened by mites may be far more vulnerable to pesticide exposure, and a habitat already fragmented by development offers less refuge from climate stress. The result is that the decline cannot be reversed by addressing any single cause alone.",
