    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The term 'infodemic'—a blend of 'information' and 'epidemic'—was popularized during the COVID-19 pandemic to describe the flood of misinformation and disinformation spreading alongside the virus itself. But scholars of communication caution that treating misinformation as a single, uniform disease can obscure more than it clarifies. Research on the spread of false claims suggests several distinct phenomena are often lumped together. Some misinformation spreads through deliberate fabrication by actors with political or financial motives. Other falsehoods arise organically from misremembered details or from the rapid sharing of unverified information during breaking events. Still others are not false at all in their factual content but are misleading because they are stripped of context or presented with exaggerated certainty. The remedies also differ: fact-checking may correct honest mistakes but does little against deliberate disinformation, which is often designed to be immune to correction. The scholars' conclusion is that effective responses must be tailored to the specific type of misinformation at issue—a single 'one-size-fits-all' intervention is unlikely to work.",
