    id: 1642,
    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The 'reproducibility crisis' refers to the growing recognition that many published scientific findings cannot be reproduced when other researchers repeat the experiments. Large-scale efforts to replicate landmark studies in psychology and cancer biology have succeeded only about half the time. The causes are structural rather than conspiratorial. Journals favor novel, positive results, creating pressure on researchers to publish striking findings; the same pressure discourages the publication of failed replications. Statistical practices contribute as well: many studies are underpowered, meaning they have too few participants to detect real effects reliably, and flexible analysis choices—such as deciding after seeing the data whether to exclude outliers—can turn noise into apparent signals. The crisis has prompted a reform movement within science. Pre-registration, in which researchers specify their hypotheses and analysis plans in advance, makes it harder to adjust methods after the fact. Open-data and open-code requirements allow other scientists to audit the analysis. And dedicated replication journals now publish null results that would previously have been lost. These reforms do not mean science is broken; they mean it is correcting itself, slowly and imperfectly, through the very scrutiny that defines the scientific method.",
