    passage: "Gentrification—the influx of wealthier residents into lower-income urban neighborhoods, often accompanied by rising rents and the displacement of long-time residents—is one of the most emotionally charged topics in urban policy, and the scholarly evidence does not yield simple verdicts. Economists who study neighborhood change point to genuine benefits: property values rise, vacant buildings are renovated, crime rates often fall, and cities gain additional tax revenue that can fund public services. Case studies also show that many original residents do not, in fact, move away; the rate of displacement is often lower than popular accounts suggest, though it varies sharply by city and by neighborhood. Yet the same research documents real harms. Affordable housing stock shrinks as rents climb, and the residents who do leave are frequently pushed into worse housing in more marginalized areas. Long-standing businesses and cultural institutions—barbershops, community centers, religious congregations—can disappear as the neighborhood's character changes. Researchers increasingly conclude that gentrification is neither an unqualified good nor an unmitigated evil, but a process whose outcomes depend heavily on policy choices: zoning rules, rent protections, and investments in affordable housing can determine whether revitalization displaces communities or includes them.",
