    passage: "Honeybees communicate the location of food sources through a remarkable behavior called the waggle dance. A returning forager performs a figure-eight pattern on the vertical surface of the honeycomb, waggling her abdomen during the straight portion of the run. The angle of the waggle run relative to vertical encodes the direction of the food source relative to the sun, and the duration of the waggle encodes the distance: the longer the waggle, the farther the food. The dance also conveys the quality of the food source—bees dance more vigorously and for longer when the nectar they found is richer. Karl von Frisch, who deciphered the dance in the 1940s, was initially met with skepticism, as many scientists doubted that insects could transmit such abstract information. Subsequent experiments using radar tracking of tagged bees confirmed that recruits could fly directly to the signaled location.",
