    id: 1655,
    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "Geologists monitor active volcanoes using a network of instruments designed to detect the warning signs of eruption. Seismometers record the small earthquakes caused by magma moving through the crust; these tremors typically increase in both frequency and intensity in the days and weeks before an eruption. Tiltmeters measure the slight swelling of the volcano's flanks as magma accumulates beneath the surface—a bulge that can grow by centimeters per day before an eruption. Gas sensors track emissions of sulfur dioxide, which rises as magma approaches the surface. GPS stations measure the slow horizontal stretching of the ground. No single signal is reliable on its own—some volcanic swarms never culminate in eruption—but when several indicators change together, the probability of an eruption rises sharply. In 1991, monitoring of these signals allowed scientists to forecast the eruption of Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines with enough warning to evacuate tens of thousands of people, saving many lives even though the eruption itself was among the largest of the twentieth century.",
