    passage: "Leafcutter ants are famous for marching in long columns, carrying pieces of leaves many times their own size back to their underground nests. But the ants do not eat the leaves. Instead, they deliver them to a fungus garden deep in the nest, where a specialized fungus breaks down the plant material into a form the ants can digest. The relationship is ancient and highly codependent: the fungus has lost the ability to survive outside the ants' care, and the ants, which lack the enzymes to digest cellulose themselves, cannot survive without the fungus. The ants maintain a strict sanitation regime, weeding out contaminant molds and treating the garden with antibiotic-producing bacteria that they carry on their bodies. Remarkably, when a young queen leaves the nest to found a new colony, she carries a small pellet of the fungus in a special pocket in her mouth, ensuring that the symbiosis continues into the next generation.",
