    passage: "Octopuses are masters of camouflage, able to change their skin color, texture, and pattern in a fraction of a second to match their surroundings. What makes this feat remarkable is the octopus's nervous system: its eyes detect the scene, but the decisions about what colors to produce appear to be made locally, in the skin itself. Each chromatophore—a pigment-filled sac in the skin—is surrounded by muscles controlled by nerves running directly from the brain. Yet studies show that an octopus with a severed nerve to one arm still camouflages that arm correctly when the rest of the body blends in, suggesting that light-sensitive proteins in the skin itself can detect color and trigger the appropriate response. Researchers have recently confirmed that octopus skin contains opsins, the same light-detecting proteins found in the retina of the eye. It appears that the octopus's skin is, in effect, a distributed light-sensing organ that can respond to its environment without waiting for instructions from the central brain.",
